{"conjecture": "Specifications compiled directly into denotational-semantic interpreters will exhibit higher fidelity to their commitments than specifications compiled to imperative code, provided the denotation functions are total and the semantic domain is computable. Specifically, if a spec is compiled to an interpreter over a Scott-continuous domain, any property proven to hold denotationally will hold at runtime, because the interpreter is literally evaluating the same denotational functions used in the proof. This collapses the gap between verification and execution by making the executable artifact a direct implementation of the mathematical semantics."}
